For single-cell technologies, a cell barcode may be present on SAM records and should be used as a grouping key during `--template-coordinate` sort. This ensures templates at the same locus are grouped together successfully when those templates are known to come from different cells.

Companion PR: https://github.com/samtools/samtools/pull/2314

@nh13 would you be willing to ensure this is added to `fgumi` too or would you welcome me to make that issue/PR?

Hello fgbio team,

I am looking for guidance on interpreting the output of CallMolecularConsensusReads. I have processed 16 UMI-tagged samples (germline and ctDNA; aimed 1000X post-deduplication) through the pipeline (**Table 1**), and I am seeing a significant discrepancy between my expected duplication rates and the final consensus yield. 

Using MarkDuplicates, the estimated duplication rate for these samples is approximately 50%. After running CallMolecularConsensusReads, I am observing a ~75% loss compared to the raw bam file (**Table 2**). I am trying to determine if this high rejection rate is a result of poor library quality or overly stringent filtering parameters. 

-What are the most common reasons for such a high proportion of reads being rejected during the consensus call beyond standard duplication?

- In my data, reads_rejected_for_insufficient_support and reads_rejected_for_orphan_consensus are both high and very similar in value. Following issue #1015, I understand orphans occur when one or both of a read pair fail due to lack of support. Given I am using the default --min-reads 2, is it common to see such high orphan counts? Could this high proportion of orphan counts arise from upstream of processing my bam files? 

-I have noticed some rejected read values are negative in the stats output. I have reported this as a potential bug in a separate issue (#1135).

-Why is the total loss (75%) so much higher than the MarkDuplicates estimate (50%)? Is this delta typically attributed to single-member families (singletons) being filtered out when --min-reads is $> 1$

-To increase retention without severely compromising base quality, which parameters should I prioritize? (e.g., --min-reads, --min-input-base-quality, or --error-rate-pre-consensus).

Sorry if some of these question were answered in previous issue and missed them.

Best,
Osvaldas

